From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase, ntdll: process and thread settings are kept and read back

GetProcessMitigationPolicy returned TRUE without writing the caller's
buffer, so Chrome and Edge read their sandbox policies back as whatever
was on their stack; SetProcessMitigationPolicy kept nothing (the two
stubs: ~1200 hits in the logs). SetProcessInformation(ProcessMemoryPriority)
failed (ProcessPagePriority was not implemented);
GetProcessInformation(ProcessPowerThrottling) was "unsupported";
GetThreadInformation was not exported; SetThreadInformation's
ThreadDynamicCodePolicy failed; ThreadPowerThrottlingState (3900 hits)
and ThreadPagePriority (1137) were stubs.

ntdll keeps a process's mitigation policies (a policy once on stays on:
turning one off is STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED), power throttling, memory
priority and leap second setting, and a thread's power throttling,
memory priority and dynamic code policy (in its private TEB data, for
any thread of the process), validating them as Windows does (version,
masks, ranges). They are kept and reported, not enforced. kernelbase's
GetProcessMitigationPolicy answers DEP from the execute flags (on and
permanent in a 64-bit process), the options mask as nothing enforced,
and the rest from ntdll, refusing bad lengths and policies with
ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER. GetProcessInformation gains the memory
priority, power throttling, leap second, protection level (none) and
app memory classes; GetThreadInformation the memory priority, power
throttling, dynamic code and absolute CPU priority classes. The wow64
thunks pass the new classes through. Headers gain the structures and
constants (PROCESS_POWER_THROTTLING_STATE, MEMORY_PRIORITY_*,
PROCESS_MITIGATION_DEP_POLICY, the newer mitigation policies...).

Wine's kernel32 process and thread tests: no new failures.

Gate: test/procsettings-gate.sh. Mutants SG_MUTANT_NO_MITIGATION_POLICY
(kernelbase/process.c), SG_MUTANT_NO_PROCESS_SETTINGS
(ntdll/unix/process.c), SG_MUTANT_NO_THREAD_SETTINGS (ntdll/unix/thread.c).
